u have to somehow  um  take into your brain but that's just not  enough right that doesn't ideas don't  you can't then sit there and sort of  logistically hammer out well therefore  it must be this way at least that  doesn't work for me so you know you  bring in all this stuff into your brain  and then it kind of rattles around in  there for a while and then something  happens and you know I can't explain  what that is I often I've had actually  graduate students you know when I go to  visit universities you have lunch with  graduate students and um and post-docs  and it's really a wonderful practice  they get to ask you all sorts of  questions and but a lot of times they'll  ask you know where do your ideas come  from you know how did you have this idea  or this idea and I I can't tell them I  mean  um  obviously most ideas have all sorts of  Precedence in the in literature people  have thought of similar ideas you know  so of course what your brain is doing is  taking these pieces that you brought in  and it's rearranging them and coming up  with something that is  um based upon all of this other stuff  but it's a new  variant right no I don't know how that  works I mean the only thing I can say is  that it works best for me when I'm not  in the lab so it's usually yeah you know  out on the hike or something like that  right when my brain is kind of  free-floating  um is when I have most of my ideas so I  I tell this to people in my lab you know  look you've got to work hard you got to  do all these things but you also you  need the downtime because that's really  when a lot of the creative stuff happens  at least for me I think everyone's  probably really different that way but  right I mean some people might do just  what you've said doesn't work for you  namely sort of  um you know really hammer out as you say  sort of the the implications of a set of  ideas  um you know and then and then some  people will do as you say you do which  is disconnect yourself from from the lab  and from sort of doing science and  you're just being a human in the world  um engaged in various activities that  somehow maybe give you time to digest or  percolate but also maybe give you  um new ways of thinking about the the  stuff that's percolating  um and I think it's really helpful for  people to know that they're you know  they they have to find what is going to  work for for their  sort of temperament or their sort of  intellectual disposition or whatever it  is that that that gets them that sort of  characterizes them being in the world so  thank you for sharing that but  um uh two final questions uh one is  given how young uh Neuroscience is as a  you know as a discipline in its own  right  um kind of how how  how do you keep up with stuff going on  around the world that's relevant to what  your work is on the assumption that  that's happening  um and then secondly  what do you want to see happen like  within the next 10 years  right well for the for to the first  um  actually  covet was a really interesting  um experience to go through because  most of us were scientists and I mean  people in general but we were sort of  cut off from our usual modes of  interacting which is we travel places we  meet other scientists face to face we  have conversations about what we're  doing they tell us about their work we  tell them about our work there's this  kind of talk about sparking creativity  it's like those personal interactions  somehow  are just way better than what we were  getting over Zoom so that's that's the  way we usually do it right and actually  the last couple of months I've been  traveling again and of course being  mindful of carbon footprint and all that  you know there's great things about you  know so it's it's complicated but um  but  obviously we can read the literature and  we can do all of those things we have to  do them to keep up with stuff but there  are you know thousands and thousands of  papers I mean there's something like no  30 000 papers a year on plasticity I  don't know I made that a number of but  there's a lot so we just can't keep up  with all of that  um so you have to be selective somehow  and then  it turns out that that's just not a very  humans are just not good at absorbing  information that way I mean we can it  can be very efficient sometimes but in  terms of like  paying attention to things and having it  spark new ideas it's really quite a bit  better to be interacting with people  um live you know so scientific meetings  are really an important way that  scientists  um interact with each other and get new  ideas you know you're learning about  stuff that's happening  long before it ever gets published so of  course it's much more on The Cutting  Edge but but it's also something about  the face-to-face interactions that I  find at least really make it  um much more effective so that's a real  dilemma I think as we're going forward  we're thinking about accessibility we're  thinking about being responsible  citizens you know it's  um it's hard for us I think like for  many  for many people to figure out how to  balance those  and then I forgot what your second  question is  um if if you could so I'll reformulate  reformulate it slightly  um  if you could have one question answered  within the next 10 years in your field  what would it be  oh my God I know it's horrible  what is consciousness  [Laughter]  that's too hard that's just too hard I I  guess  um I feel so it's one reason it's hard  is that  I don't think  um I think the way we're gonna  understand the brain is  slowly and sort of um holistically like  we're not gonna understand one piece of  it and then not the rest of it because  the problems are all the same problems  right okay so I think the field is going  to keep progressing but in a way that is  um  I mean we're really groping towards  trying to understand something as  complicated as the brain is  and so uh  you know I guess  if there was one thing I would let's say  I would love to have figured out maybe  by the time  um  you know by the time I retire I guess it  would be  um well it'd be really cool to  understand say  um what the mechanism is by which sleep  can switch homeostatic plasticity on and  off and control the direction  um that would be really pretty cool to  understand and I would really love to  understand how  these processes that we've been studying  for a long time how they actually enable  our brains to  um  to store  lots of information so you know we think  these homeostatic mechanisms probably  increase the capacity of of networks or  memory storage that's sort of good  theoretical reasons to think that based  on a lot of other people's Theory  um Theory work so it'd be great if we  could figure out why that is so those  are sort of two threads that are going  on in the lab now that are pretty  exciting  um but overall these are like tiny that  they're sort of big but they're also  sort of tiny problems that I think  that's where everyone's at right now in  Brain Research like we we hopefully are  going to start to get a handle on some  of these very fundamental processes  once we do that then I think we can  start to tackle some of the bigger  questions  oh lovely well I I hope that within the  next 10 years I'll be reading about your  answers to to both of those questions  and we'll be able to as you say move on  to um larger and larger questions uh but  in the meantime thank you so much I  appreciate your time and expertise  um and I know everyone is going to be  um very feel very lucky and be very  lucky to uh have heard from you directly  so thank you Dr Gina triggiano for your  time and  um we I hope that we will get to talk to  you again someday

